<DocLayout
  title="Agents & runs"
  description="Build and Plan modes, approvals, checkpoints, diffs, worktrees, the task queue, and the emergency stop in SAARTHI-STUDIO."
>
  <p>
    An agent in SAARTHI-STUDIO is a bounded run loop, not an autonomous
    process you lose control of. Every step is visible, privileged
    actions stop for approval, and you keep the final decision.
  </p>

  <h2>Build and Plan</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Build</strong> — the agent answers and makes changes:
      edits files and runs commands, pausing for approval on each
      privileged step.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Plan</strong> — the agent replies with an implementation
      plan and makes no changes. Useful for scoping before you commit.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Approvals and permissions</h2>
  <p>
    Writes and commands are gated by permission rules. When a step needs
    approval, the run waits; you inspect the proposed action and allow
    or deny it. Agent commands run with your user privileges — they are
    not OS-sandboxed — so the approval gate is the boundary, and the
    audit trail records what ran.
  </p>

  <h2>Checkpoints, diffs, worktrees</h2>
  <p>
    Runs keep checkpoints you can restore, and every file change is
    reviewable as a diff. Writing runs can work in an isolated Git
    worktree: your main checkout stays untouched until you commit,
    merge, or apply the run's result yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>The task queue</h2>
  <p>
    Queue tasks with dependencies and retries; the Runs view shows each
    run's step log, token usage, and outcome. A run interrupted mid-step
    is recorded as interrupted, never silently completed.
  </p>

  <h2>Stopping</h2>
  <p>
    Every streaming reply has a working Stop button (the partial answer
    is saved), and a global emergency stop halts agent activity. Closing
    the window shuts down the local server and stops the router only if
    this run started it.
  </p>

  <h2>Honest limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Commands are not OS-sandboxed; approvals and permission rules are the control surface.</li>
    <li>The agent works inside the folder you opened — paths outside it are refused.</li>
    <li>Run quality depends on the model you connect; see <a href={withBase('/docs/providers/')}>Providers &amp; models</a>.</li>
  </ul>
</DocLayout>
